00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.100
So let's get into it.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:59.420
Streams of events everywhere--
we're getting increasingly good

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:03.140
at capturing interactions
with devices, digitizing them.

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.460
So this is no secret.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:08.960
And as engineers, we're
increasingly often challenged

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.350
with turning these streams of
events into useful features--

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.150
useful, interesting,
entertaining things

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.440
for our users.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.350
So, for example, in
gaming and media,

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.360
we're using in-session
user activity

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.420
to adjust gameplay
as the users play.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.490
We're adjusting content
based on browsing history.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.770
And we're certainly
adjusting the advertisements

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.510
as they show up.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.487
Now, in e-commerce--
well, first of all,

00:03:31.487 --> 00:03:33.320
if you're in e-commerce,
you're already also

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.300
are in the e-commerce
and media business--

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.450
but you're also building
recommendation systems, right?

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.660
And so, the recommendations
that you show me on page two

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:45.400
had better know what I just
did on page one 15 seconds ago.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.900
In finance, this has, of course,
been a longstanding problem

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:51.500
where, let's say, the risk group
needs to aggregate events that

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.965
are trades in various
departments of a bank

00:03:54.965 --> 00:03:57.830
as well as all the
market activity that's

00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.020
happening outside.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:04.140
So what's new is
the sheer scale.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.040
Now, it used to be--

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:08.970
so people say,
maybe 20 years ago,

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.265
you could have a very
successful large business with

00:04:11.265 --> 00:04:13.920
a 1,000-person call center
generating 100 events per

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.709
second.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:16.500
Any messaging middleware
kept up with that,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.480
and things were great.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.269
Zoom to now, and I'm
routinely speaking

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.820
to customers of GCP
ingesting thousands,

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.600
hundreds of thousands,
millions of events a second

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.660
and doing something useful
with them in real time.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.730
So what we need is not
just messaging middleware

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.190
but a high-throughput
ingestion and delivery system

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.260
for streams of events to
make sense of all this.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.380
And, of course,
Kafka has sort of

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.520
become the de facto standard
of this kind of system

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.170
in the open-source community.

00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.510
So I wanted to take
a couple of minutes

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.550
to just walk you
through some of the kind

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.260
of prototypical
patterns that I see

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:59.340
when I talk to users of Kafka
in Cloud Pub/Sub on an example

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.310
of a sort of a hypothetical
e-commerce business that

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.400
got its hands on a
high-throughput ingestion

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.120
and delivery system
for streams of events.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.380
So the journey usually
starts with collecting

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.170
user interaction
events for analysis.

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.272
If you're going to create
a useful experience

00:05:15.272 --> 00:05:17.730
for your customers, you need
to have your analysts and data

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:21.000
scientists go to the
warehouse and actually

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.830
figure out what's working.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.510
And so the way we
build this is, I'm

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.700
shopping for shorts
and flip-flops,

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.330
so I'm viewing the shorts
page on my mobile device.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.680
I tap on the shorts.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:34.440
That generates a request
to stateless front-end.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.330
That front-end is
stateless because it's

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:40.710
able to take advantage of a
high-throughput event ingestion

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:42.222
API to persist the event.

00:05:42.222 --> 00:05:43.680
It just publishes
it right through.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.020
It no longer has to worry
about writing it to disk.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.770
It no longer has to
worry about writing it

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:50.430
to the database and
returns to the user.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:56.580
On the other side, now we use
the delivery side of the API

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.014
to start building
processing pipelines.

00:05:59.014 --> 00:06:00.930
So we'll start with the
Dataflow pipeline that

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.480
just grabs the events,
parses them, turns them

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.572
into structured data, puts them
in the warehouse-- so BigQuery.

00:06:06.572 --> 00:06:09.030
Of course, soon enough, you
realize that you don't actually

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:10.000
need all the data.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.880
You need some
aggregates or filtering.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.650
So you just keep
adding these pipelines

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.510
as you understand what you need.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.620
The beautiful thing is, your
front-ends are stateless.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:21.030
You can add your back-ends
whenever you wish,

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.150
because, in the middle,
there's this thing that

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.310
is able to scale
with the throughput

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:28.420
and decouple the two systems.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.990
So once you've done this,
your data scientists

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.810
make their first discovery.

00:06:32.810 --> 00:06:35.340
And you're ready to create
your first feedback loop.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:39.840
They discover that users tend to
buy things they've seen before.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.740
And so what we want to build
is a simple feature where,

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.240
when I'm browsing
for shorts, I want

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.740
to see the history
of all the shorts

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.420
that I've seen before, right--

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.040
just a strip of my user history.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.200
So we will build that a
recently viewed service.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.250
Every time a request comes
in for a product page,

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:59.520
the front-ends reach into the
service with a user profile

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.480
and get back a list of
products I've browsed.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.170
Perfect.

00:07:04.170 --> 00:07:04.950
Very simple.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.370
The question is, how do
we populate user history?

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:10.770
And that's simple as well--
just another Dataflow pipeline.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.020
A browsing event comes
in-- a page view.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:16.770
You grab the user ID, product
ID, stuff it into a big table.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:19.070
Now you have a profile.

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:21.450
So this is working.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:22.590
Your revenue is going up.

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:23.180
You're happy.

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:24.920
Your boss is happy.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.070
You're ready to take the next
step, which will illustrate

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.380
another kind of common pattern,
which is handling events that

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.600
are just changes in a database.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:35.220
We're doing change data capture.

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.190
And so, the goal now
is to capture changes

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.770
in a database of
an existing service

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.600
and materialize them
in something else.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:45.990
So what we want to do now
is build an experience

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:47.670
where, when I'm
browsing for shorts,

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.290
I get a recommendation
for flip-flops,

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:52.292
because they're relevant--
not my browsing history.

00:07:52.292 --> 00:07:54.000
So we build a
you-might-like-this service

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.770
that has a product graph.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:58.680
You give it a product ID, it
gives you other product IDs.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:01.320
We don't get this from the
customers' activity stream,

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:03.180
but we might have an
inventory management

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.100
service or some
other similar system

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:09.720
that your buyers use to sort
of select onboard products

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:10.980
to your e-commerce store.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.040
And they're writing content.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.450
They may be saying, this
comes in such-and-such color.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.940
It's such-and-such brand.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.380
This is starting to
look like useful data

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:22.610
that you can use to
build a product graph.

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:28.720
Accept that the database is
provisioned for your buyers.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:31.700
These are the 10 people who are
responsible for what products

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:34.540
are sold, not the million
end-users you have.

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:38.270
So to deal with that problem,
we capture the change stream

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:41.809
from the product database,
turn it into events.

00:08:41.809 --> 00:08:44.570
And from there, we
can build any number

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:48.290
of materialized views of that,
such as the product graph.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:49.920
So these are the
common patterns.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.430
Now out of habit,
I've been showing you

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:55.950
GCP hexagons, Cloud Pub/Sub,
my first product at Google.

00:08:55.950 --> 00:08:58.370
So forgive me, but, of
course, in reality, Kafka

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.590
does all of these things
and, increasingly, not

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:03.330
just ingestion and delivery,
but also stream processing

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:05.480
with K streams and KSQL.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:08.467
So the question is, why would
you want to use Kafka and GCP,

00:09:08.467 --> 00:09:09.050
and would you?

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.380
Well, it turns out that
the answer to this question

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.700
is, there are at least
three reasons why you would.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:15.980
And there are many others.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:19.230
But it turns out to be
often the right choice.

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:23.510
So reason number one
is easy migration.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:25.850
You're rarely migrating
to Cloud because you're

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.399
unhappy with your Kafka Cluster.

00:09:27.399 --> 00:09:28.940
You're probably
migrating because you

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:31.430
want to change your
warehouse, or maybe you

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:34.640
want to take advantage of
Google Kubernetes engine

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.580
to run your
application front-ends.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:40.970
Kafka will migrate
happily and live in Cloud.

00:09:40.970 --> 00:09:45.230
So no reason to change what
doesn't need to be changed.

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:48.710
Second is, if you take a step
back, when you start migrating,

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:51.062
you're probably building
a hybrid environment.

00:09:51.062 --> 00:09:53.270
Maybe that's part of your
sort of long-term migration

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:54.020
strategy.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:56.300
Maybe that's the end-state
where you want to land.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:00.980
And so, in this case, you want
your data exchange and access

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.380
layer to be something that
works both On-premises

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.090
and across public clouds.

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:07.100
So a native service,
like Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:09.420
is not the natural
solution in this case.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.660
And as you will see, Kafka and
Confluent Cloud, in particular,

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.360
are very good at this problem.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.890
And then finally, if you're
doing event sourcing,

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:24.830
this Kafka is absolutely
the right starting place.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:27.350
Event sourcing takes advantage
of Kafka's particular way

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:30.230
of doing event ingestion
and delivery, which

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:32.720
is it's a complete log.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:34.520
It allows you to
store events as they

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:37.190
come in in an append-only
log and then serve them out

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:37.869
in order.

00:10:37.869 --> 00:10:39.410
When you have a
complete log that you

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:42.660
can read with high throughput
many, many times over,

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.410
you can use it to store
the set of changes

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:49.030
to a state or database and
then materialize that database

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:51.660
as many times as you
want without putting

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.490
a load on your source
database, as you

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.490
might do if you, for
example, were experimenting

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:58.160
with something that takes a lot
of different materializations

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.740
like an Elasticsearch cluster.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:04.700
So event sourcing, hybrid
systems, and quick migrations

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:08.480
are three very good reasons
to start with Kafka on GCP.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:12.530
Of course, as you succeed,
now you have a problem.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:15.550
You have to run Kafka well.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:21.810
Running Kafka well turns out to
be hard as really any system.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:23.320
But I'm a product
manager, remember.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.130
I don't get to run
anything in production.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.990
They don't let me.

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:30.400
What I do get to do is I do
get to meet the people who do.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.200
So I'd like to invite
Boerge to tell you

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.060
of his experience running
Kafka at the New York Times.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:40.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:41.780
BOERGE SVINGEN: So thank you.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:47.770
So what we're using
Kafka for at the Times

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:54.570
is making published content
available to our readers.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:57.840
And this is what
that looks like.

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.710
So we have a blog post out that
describes how that actually

00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:02.470
works.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:07.400
I'm not going to go
into detail on that now,

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:11.730
only to say that this is an
example of advanced sourcing

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:15.970
or, specifically, a
log-based architecture.

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:19.960
So the core principle in
this is that we maintain

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:24.910
a log of every asset published
by The New York Times

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:31.450
since the beginning of it
in '51 and up until events

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:35.860
being published in real
time and now, I hope,

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:37.390
since we're all here this week.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:45.880
There are two main principles
for running such a log.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:48.910
We need two main requirements.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:55.030
We need ordered playback,
because the published

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:59.410
events we are writing to the
log have causal relationships

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:00.560
between them.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.250
So the ordering matters.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:07.240
And the other thing is that
we need infinite retention.

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:11.660
We want this to be
available forever.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:18.340
And only Kafka fulfills
both of those requirements.

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:22.900
Now, because Kafka is
such a critical piece

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:28.720
of our infrastructure, the
news doesn't get it out here

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:30.520
if our Kafka cluster is down.

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:34.780
And that's a very big
deal at the Times.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:37.750
So because of that, we put
a lot of work and effort

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:42.740
into keeping this Kafka
cluster available and reliable.

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:48.170
So we have a dedicated team
of engineers running it.

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:54.760
And more concretely,
we're running this on GCE.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:14:01.480
We might have chosen GKE
if we had started over now.

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:06.550
We have an Ansible script
that handles all our Kafka

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:13.340
deployments and rolling
updates and all of that.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:19.370
We have set up multi-region
replication using MirrorMaker,

00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:22.610
so everything published
to one Kafka cluster

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:27.170
becomes available everywhere.

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:32.750
Latency is very important
to us since this is news.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:36.020
So we also have
implemented our own log

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:41.510
offset monitor that lets us know
if one of these log consumers

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:44.220
fall behind.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:48.800
And we're also taking full
advantage of Kafka's ACLs,

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:52.160
specifically to limit
rights, because anything

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:55.540
being written to
this Kafka cluster

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:58.700
goes directly onto the site.

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:00.470
So we have to keep
very tight control

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:04.370
on who gets to write to it.

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:05.990
In addition to
all of this, we're

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:09.840
also very conservative in
how we use the cluster.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:13.280
So, for instance, we don't
allow other teams at the Times

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:18.050
to use this Kafka cluster
for their own things.

00:15:18.050 --> 00:15:20.150
And as a consequence
of that, using things

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:23.450
like Kafka Streams,
KSQL, and Kafka Connect

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:29.540
is a lot harder than
we would want it to be.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:31.790
And in spite of all
of this, things still

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:33.670
sometimes go wrong.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:36.320
So Kafka is highly
available in the sense

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:40.670
that, if a broker goes
down, things are still fine.

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.960
But when all the brokers are
logging excessively and run out

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:48.270
of disk at the same time,
that doesn't really help.

00:15:48.270 --> 00:15:51.560
It also didn't help us when
we configured mutual SSL

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:56.030
authentication and all our old
communication between our Kafka

00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:00.920
brokers stopped very
suddenly, which also brought

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.900
the entire cluster down.

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:06.170
Maybe the most frightening
case we've seen

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:10.840
was when one of our consumers
had its offset reset

00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:14.060
and immediately started creating
a version of the New York Times

00:16:14.060 --> 00:16:19.070
home page with old
news, which is really

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:20.120
not something we want.

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:23.240
Luckily, we managed to stop that
before it got to the reader.

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:25.940
But we do remember that day.

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:37.380
And basically, as a technology
strategy at the Times,

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:41.380
we want to run managed
services whenever we can.

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:43.240
We want to be good at news.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:47.100
We don't want to be good
at running infrastructure.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:52.630
And the reason we run
our own Kafka at all

00:16:52.630 --> 00:16:57.590
and put in all the time and
resources and attention to that

00:16:57.590 --> 00:17:01.170
is because, when we started
this project two years ago,

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:04.510
there was no managed
Kafka on GCP,

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:09.450
which I had long
discussions with Kir about.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:11.850
Gwen?

00:17:11.850 --> 00:17:15.055
GWEN SHAPIRA: So some people
want to be good at news and not

00:17:15.055 --> 00:17:16.490
at running Kafka.

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:18.569
We, on the other
hand, at Confluent

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:19.950
are kind of crappy at news.

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:23.250
On the other hand, we're very,
very good at running Kaffa.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:26.880
We've been running Kafka
in production our team

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:30.890
since the first lines
of code for Kafka

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:33.450
were ever written at LinkedIn.

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:38.790
About a year and change ago,
we announced Confluent Cloud

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:44.200
as a way to let us manage your
Kafka in the public cloud.

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:47.570
Now in order to really see the
value of what we're doing here,

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:51.000
I want to take you
a few years back--

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:56.120
back to the days where Kafka
was first invented at LinkedIn.

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:59.150
10 years ago, we started
seeing the microservices trend.

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:02.490
And the more microservices
we had, the more databases

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:05.550
each microservice had, the
more lines of communication

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:08.850
going back and forth between
microservices and database,

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:12.270
our entire infrastructure
became very complex, very

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:13.140
hard to manage.

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:16.110
Now this talk is not
about simplifying

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:18.240
the complexity of IT,
even though that's

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:19.980
a good topic of discussion.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:25.850
It's about how Kafka can
help here and really simplify

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:27.430
your architecture.

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:31.830
So when you run
Apache Kafka, you

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:35.370
can now have connections between
the different applications.

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.610
In Kafka, every event
that is published to Kafka

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:40.890
is available to everything.

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:42.510
And this obviously
greatly simplifies.

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:44.640
You build a centralized
architecture

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:46.770
that allows you to
deploy new services

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:49.490
and give them access very
easily to all the data

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:53.430
that your organization has
at scale in a very reliable

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:55.470
distributed system.

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:58.080
Now, of course, the messy
infrastructure-- once

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:01.040
we started doing cloud
migrations and hybrid cloud,

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:02.794
it did not get any better.

00:19:02.794 --> 00:19:03.960
In fact, it got a lot worse.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:05.959
We took the mess that we
had in one data center,

00:19:05.959 --> 00:19:08.250
and now we have a mess
in five data centers

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:11.024
and in this cloud provider and
in the other cloud provider.

00:19:11.024 --> 00:19:12.690
And now that it's
going on public cloud,

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:16.660
it has real costs, and it
has a real security risk.

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:20.860
So the mess just got
a lot more expensive.

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:23.480
So think about the
same architectures

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:26.820
that simplified our
microservices deployment

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:30.060
OnPrem now applied for
both cloud migration

00:19:30.060 --> 00:19:31.290
and in the cloud.

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:34.620
It really allows you to have
one channel of communication

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:35.340
to secure.

00:19:35.340 --> 00:19:39.270
Each event is replicated
once, which drives down costs,

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:42.810
and you have this nice, much
easier to manage and monitor

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:44.310
architecture out there.

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:45.300
But that's not it.

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:48.420
Because you have all the data
available in Kafka 4 streams,

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.950
you can start experimenting
with everything

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:52.410
the clouds have to offer.

00:19:52.410 --> 00:19:54.720
You want to try Dataflow.

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:56.040
You want to try TensorFlow.

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:57.600
You want to try BigQuery.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:00.510
Even if you never did it before,
you're totally [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:04.080
very easy to just
stream events from Kafka

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:06.310
to BigQuery and
run an experiment.

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:10.050
So you really have a lot
more possibility out there.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:13.620
And this is really divisions
that inspired Apache Kafka.

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:16.710
Connect everything to
everything from OnPrem

00:20:16.710 --> 00:20:19.590
to the Cloud, microservices
and databases,

00:20:19.590 --> 00:20:23.220
and make all the data available
to the entire organization--

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:26.820
kind of breaking down
all those silos vision.

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:29.370
But when building
a platform, it's

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.560
not just about the
platform itself.

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:34.590
That's a lesson that we
all learned from eBay.

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:36.210
You build a platform
to sell stuff,

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:39.210
but the point is the
stuff you actually sell.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:42.000
The power of this
centralized platform

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.070
is from the things that
connect to the platform that

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:49.410
bring in the data and create
value to the business.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:51.450
And the value is really
in the microservices

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:53.710
and the ecosystem.

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:57.120
So Confluent includes
a lot of components

00:20:57.120 --> 00:21:01.710
that make it very easy
to create those very

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:04.590
lively ecosystems that
make data available

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:06.010
for your organization.

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:09.630
One of the coolest ones
is Schema Registry,

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:13.140
which allows you to make changes
to the schema of the events,

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:16.500
to the data that you are
transmitting, independently

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:19.780
between the producer of
the data and the consumer.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:21.780
So the producer wants
to make some changes,

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.400
and you can do it
in very safe ways.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:28.730
Agile is all about making
change and evolution safe.

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:30.530
Another cool tool is KSQL.

00:21:30.530 --> 00:21:32.370
I think Boerge mentioned it.

00:21:32.370 --> 00:21:35.640
It allows you to create
stream processing applications

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.710
using a very familiar
SQL-like language.

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:41.250
We also have clients in
pretty much every language.

00:21:41.250 --> 00:21:42.790
You're seeing like eight here.

00:21:42.790 --> 00:21:44.880
There's like 50 of
them that you can use.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:47.950
If you like PHP, here
go Kafka PHP clients.

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:50.240
That's kind of the
power of open-source

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:52.140
is that, once you
have an open protocol,

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:53.430
everyone can create clients.

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:57.300
You're not depending on
anyone to do it for you.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:01.740
Now, we mentioned databases,
and integrating microservices

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:03.980
and databases is
really what allows

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:05.870
you to run your entire
organization off

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:07.610
of one stream of events.

00:22:07.610 --> 00:22:10.550
Kafka has the Kafka
Connect infrastructure.

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:13.520
And Kafka Connect allows
you to plug in connectors.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:15.740
And we have 100-plus
connectors allowing

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:18.350
you to get data from different
databases into in Kafka,

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:21.920
from Kafka to other databases,
from Kafka to another Kafka.

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:25.760
If you want data from Vertica
to show up in Big Query--

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:28.380
people actually do it-- you
have a Vertica connector getting

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.220
data one way, BigQuery
connector the other way,

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:32.700
and you are good to go.

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:34.580
This is incredibly
powerful, especially

00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:39.660
since it requires almost no
coding, and we run it for you.

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:41.510
So that's the
whole thing, right?

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:42.492
You want to use it.

00:22:42.492 --> 00:22:43.700
You want to get at your data.

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.350
You want to build
your microservices.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:48.414
You don't want to
actually run any of it.

00:22:48.414 --> 00:22:50.330
And the reason you don't
want to run any of it

00:22:50.330 --> 00:22:52.650
is that Kafka is a
distributed system.

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:56.510
And running distributed systems
is difficult. We should know.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:58.790
Writing distributed systems
was difficult enough

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:00.150
in the first place.

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:01.500
It's kind of tricky.

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:06.020
But as we all know, debugging
is twice as hard as coding.

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:08.960
You're not allowed to be as
smart as humanly possible while

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:12.320
coding, because you need to be
twice as smart while debugging

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:12.980
something.

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:15.460
Raise conditions and
scheduler timing--

00:23:15.460 --> 00:23:19.730
all this has been a thorn in
our side for a very long time.

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:22.840
Now operations is all
the fun of debugging

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:25.440
with time pressure,
customers yelling

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:27.440
at you, money ticking away.

00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:28.940
What can be more fun?

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:33.260
So I want to just show you three
reasons that it's a good idea

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:35.950
to let someone else
run Kafka for you.

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:39.440
The first is that
random stuff happens.

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:43.380
And random stuff is usually
not that good stuff.

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:45.280
There are the failures
that we expect to see.

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:48.726
We kind of know that this will
fail, and machines may fail,

00:23:48.726 --> 00:23:50.100
and the network
will have issues.

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:51.830
Those we plan for.

00:23:51.830 --> 00:23:55.940
People who forget to renew
their SSL certificates,

00:23:55.940 --> 00:23:57.920
not so much planned for.

00:23:57.920 --> 00:24:01.580
But the thing is that,
when you have a strong team

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:05.090
and you build a strong system
that is capable of overcoming

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:06.770
failures, we have
the experience,

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:09.470
and you have the tools, and you
have the monitoring in place,

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:11.390
you can build very
resilient systems.

00:24:11.390 --> 00:24:14.570
The trick is that this requires
experience and expertise.

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:15.710
You can build it in-house.

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:16.760
And most people do.

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:19.190
Boerge has an amazing
team that managed to build

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:21.360
something very resilient.

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:26.200
But, as you said, it may not
be your business to do that.

00:24:26.200 --> 00:24:28.220
Even when nothing
absolutely fails,

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:31.050
you still have to make
changes to the system.

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:33.290
You can't just say,
"It works today.

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:35.720
Let's not touch it ever again."

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:37.880
Because even though there
is a risk of change--

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:41.340
every upgrade has a risk of new
bugs and things going wrong--

00:24:41.340 --> 00:24:43.697
there's also a risk of
not changing, right?

00:24:43.697 --> 00:24:45.530
We know that, if you
don't evolve and adapt,

00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:46.970
you'll be left behind.

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:48.710
New features
actually have values.

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:51.980
They allow you to
implement things faster.

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:54.620
Some people are kind of sad
that they cannot use Kafka,

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:57.320
because they know that it
will allow them to move faster

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:58.790
and deliver value faster.

00:24:58.790 --> 00:25:01.070
Why not offload
the risk to someone

00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:03.860
who is being paid to take
this risk and go on your way?

00:25:03.860 --> 00:25:05.390
You'll get the benefits.

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:06.710
And this is really the key--

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:10.130
you focus on your ideas,
we focus on running Kafka,

00:25:10.130 --> 00:25:11.670
and everything will be amazing.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:17.360
So I want to jump into a
demo, a kind of semi-live demo

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:20.870
where I show you how I actually
build one of the architectures

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.740
that Kir mentioned, streaming
data and joining it and doing

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:28.160
some processing to
it using Confluent

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.410
Cloud on multiple clouds.

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:34.180
So it will be a
hybrid cloud demo.

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:35.950
OK, so you can see here--

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:38.660
I have two clusters
in Confluent Cloud.

00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:40.180
This is the Confluent Cloud UI.

00:25:40.180 --> 00:25:42.720
You can see I have AWS and GCP.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:45.010
I'm going to move
data between them.

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:47.380
You can see how big
my clusters are--

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:48.340
huge, right?

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:50.410
OK, those are demo clusters.

00:25:50.410 --> 00:25:51.880
I want to create a new cluster.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:53.800
I can choose a
name, and then I can

00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:56.590
choose how large or small
the cluster is going to be,

00:25:56.590 --> 00:25:58.990
which cloud it's going to be on.

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:01.330
And you can see that,
as I change my cluster

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:05.080
configuration, the pricing on
the right kind of automatically

00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:07.204
adjusts to reflect
my new cluster.

00:26:07.204 --> 00:26:08.620
If you pay a lot
of attention, you

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:13.980
can see that GCP is slightly
cheaper than the competitors.

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:15.732
Once I'm happy with
the configuration,

00:26:15.732 --> 00:26:17.440
you can see that, if
I want more storage,

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:20.890
it's going to be slightly more
expensive than cases where

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:22.719
I want less storage.

00:26:22.719 --> 00:26:23.760
I find the configuration.

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:24.801
I'm happy with the price.

00:26:24.801 --> 00:26:26.320
I'm happy with my set-up.

00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:26.990
I go in.

00:26:26.990 --> 00:26:28.900
In this case, I'm canceling.

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:33.261
And let's see how you manage
those clusters that I now got.

00:26:33.261 --> 00:26:35.260
So you can see here--
this is the list of topics

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:37.870
that I already have
in my GCP cluster.

00:26:37.870 --> 00:26:40.780
And then, if I go to AWS, you
can see the list of topics

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:43.130
that I have in AWS cluster.

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:46.159
And suppose that I want to
see what is in the topic.

00:26:46.159 --> 00:26:47.200
I can look at the schema.

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:49.260
I mentioned the Schema Registry.

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:50.890
And that allows me
to keep compatible.

00:26:50.890 --> 00:26:53.380
You can see that, in
this topic, I have users,

00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:55.840
and you have the
usual suspect fields.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:59.770
I have to User ID and Region
and Gender and kind of the stuff

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:02.240
I want to know
about those users.

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:06.790
Now suppose that I have
those users in AWS.

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:10.210
And maybe they
arrived from something

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:12.280
that dumps that in S3.

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:13.900
I also have maybe
some page views.

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:15.920
I have a bunch of
stuff running on AWS.

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:20.440
But I really want to process it
on GCP, because GCP has things

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:22.850
like TensorFlow and BigQuery--

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:24.610
a lot of stuff that
I really enjoy using.

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:27.130
As a developer, you always
have your favorite tools.

00:27:27.130 --> 00:27:29.890
And some of them
happen to be in GCP.

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:33.320
Getting data from cloud to cloud
is actually a hard problem.

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:35.320
This is something
that you think is

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:38.500
going to be easy until you
basically go and try to do it,

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:40.210
and you figure out
that, if all you know

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:42.790
is the stuff within your
cloud, that's not going

00:27:42.790 --> 00:27:44.870
to help you move anywhere else.

00:27:44.870 --> 00:27:47.800
Confluent Replicator that
is based on Kafka Connect

00:27:47.800 --> 00:27:49.870
basically allows you
to copy some data.

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:53.530
So you go into Kafka
Connect, and you choose

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:55.070
what topics you want to copy.

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:57.070
So you can have a
whitelist, a blacklist,

00:27:57.070 --> 00:27:58.620
may be everything
except the test

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:00.730
topics, a regular expression.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:03.910
You can basically type in the
topics that you want to copy.

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:07.240
And then you can see a bit
down you have, where does that

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:07.780
come from.

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:10.270
In this case, it comes from AWS.

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:13.240
And a bit farther
down, a lot of security

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:17.110
configs, and it's going to be,
where is the data going to?

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:19.900
It's going to GCP.

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:22.960
Oh, more configuration
to talk through--

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:24.650
I can rename the topics.

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:29.320
So I can say that the topic used
to be called AWS.users I want

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:31.880
to rename it to start with GCP.

00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:34.224
So it will kind of
make a bit more sense

00:28:34.224 --> 00:28:35.890
and know where the
data came from, where

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:38.420
it went to, what is the topic.

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:41.730
Those hierarchical topic
names are kind of common.

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:44.080
Once I'm happy with
the config, I just

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:48.230
go ahead, start this Replicator.

00:28:48.230 --> 00:28:52.280
And let's go and see
the topics in GCP.

00:28:52.280 --> 00:28:53.330
So here we go.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:57.100
We did the rename, so
it's GCP.AWS.users.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:58.660
Now, the first thing
I want to check

00:28:58.660 --> 00:29:00.370
is that my schema
arrived intact,

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:04.090
that my data is more or less
the way I expect it to be.

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:07.450
So I go in and say,
browse schema, once again.

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:09.610
And you kind of say,
yep, looks the same.

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:12.550
I have the region, the gender,
everything that I expected.

00:29:12.550 --> 00:29:16.960
Now, let's go back and see
what is the data actually like.

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:18.616
So we go to a different topic.

00:29:18.616 --> 00:29:19.990
Remember, Replicator
two topics--

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:22.020
the page views and my users.

00:29:22.020 --> 00:29:24.880
Let's go to the page
views and use KSQL to see

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:26.890
what is the data in my topic.

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:30.040
So I do query using KSQL.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:32.980
And the first thing
I have to do is

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:35.350
create a stream, which is
basically a materialized view

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:36.210
on top of the topic.

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:40.490
I have one already
existing, so no big deal.

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:42.490
And once again, you can
see it's a very simple.

00:29:42.490 --> 00:29:45.460
I do select*, run the query.

00:29:45.460 --> 00:29:48.070
Behind the scenes, it starts
a stream processing job,

00:29:48.070 --> 00:29:49.150
the consumer.

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:53.050
And pretty soon, you'll see
a stream of events on screen.

00:29:53.050 --> 00:29:54.550
And this is a live
stream of events.

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:57.950
You can see the user ID and
the page the user is viewing.

00:29:57.950 --> 00:29:59.350
You can see the timestamp.

00:29:59.350 --> 00:30:02.020
And as data keeps
coming into the topic,

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:05.064
you can see that I have more
and more events to go through.

00:30:05.064 --> 00:30:06.730
You can see this is
a pretty busy topic.

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:08.470
So you can see that
we are accumulating

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:10.930
more and more events in there.

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:13.540
Now, this is kind
of a simple query.

00:30:13.540 --> 00:30:16.300
Suppose that I want a
subset of the events.

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:20.020
Suppose that they want only
page views from a single gender

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:21.220
in the audience.

00:30:21.220 --> 00:30:25.330
Well, the page view topic does
not have anything about gender.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:26.650
But it has user ID.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:29.620
And the user topic has
the gender of each user.

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:32.290
So I can join those
two streams of events

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:35.440
and then do a filter
based on the other field,

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:36.650
very normal thing.

00:30:36.650 --> 00:30:38.380
This is a continuous query.

00:30:38.380 --> 00:30:41.055
So it's a query that
basically does an ETL

00:30:41.055 --> 00:30:42.790
job that runs all the time.

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:45.100
The results go into a new topic.

00:30:45.100 --> 00:30:46.780
Now what do I do
with the results that

00:30:46.780 --> 00:30:48.280
go in your new topic?

00:30:48.280 --> 00:30:49.850
Well, I may want
to analyze them.

00:30:49.850 --> 00:30:53.450
That's why I ended up in
GCP, in order to analyze it.

00:30:53.450 --> 00:30:56.020
So what I want to do
is take those events,

00:30:56.020 --> 00:31:00.940
dump them into, say,
BigQuery, and now

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:02.160
the sky is the limit, right?

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:05.380
You can use Google Data Studio,
and there's tons of tools.

00:31:05.380 --> 00:31:08.020
So I go again to Kafka
Connect and create

00:31:08.020 --> 00:31:11.800
a sink connector that uses
Google BigQuery as a connector.

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:13.480
So I pick the topic--

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:16.120
the subset of page views
that I already created.

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:18.200
I pick the BigQuery connector.

00:31:18.200 --> 00:31:21.280
I give it a name--
say, Google BigQuery.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:23.800
And then I start
configuring this topic.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:25.950
And you can see that,
if you use BigQuery,

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:28.090
those configurations
should be super familiar.

00:31:28.090 --> 00:31:30.730
Which tables do I want
the topics to go into?

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:32.129
What is the project?

00:31:32.129 --> 00:31:32.920
What are the views?

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:35.410
All those things that
I've already used to sink.

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:38.650
Once I'm happy with my
configuration, I click OK,

00:31:38.650 --> 00:31:39.640
and I'm done.

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:40.820
And think about it--

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:42.400
this was a seven-minute demo.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:47.340
We got data out of AWS,
into GCP, queried it,

00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:49.490
created a continuous
transformation,

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:53.180
and dumped the data into
BigQuery into further analysis.

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:55.000
So this is kind of--

00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:56.590
if I may say so myself--

00:31:56.590 --> 00:31:57.550
kind of a big deal.

00:31:57.550 --> 00:31:59.560
It used to be
incredibly difficult,

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:01.360
because I know I've
been trying to do it

00:32:01.360 --> 00:32:04.710
for a pretty long time.

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:07.040
So this is really
one part of the value

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:10.490
of having an open-source,
open-standard software is

00:32:10.490 --> 00:32:12.560
that you are not
locked into a vendor.

00:32:12.560 --> 00:32:15.410
You can deploy and have the
same experience in every cloud,

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:19.280
and you can easily move
between the clouds.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:22.910
Now, if you remember two
things from this presentation,

00:32:22.910 --> 00:32:25.910
the first is that streaming
platform gives a lot of value

00:32:25.910 --> 00:32:29.180
to the business, and data
sharing gives a lot of value

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:30.020
to the business.

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:32.210
But it really takes an
ecosystem of tools that

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:33.910
exists around Apache Kafka.

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:37.050
You cannot do it with
just a messaging system.

00:32:37.050 --> 00:32:38.780
You need a lot more than that.

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:41.300
And that, if you don't want to
go through the pain of having

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:44.840
to learn how to manage it
yourself, which takes a while,

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:49.220
you may want to run Confluent
Cloud on public cloud

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:50.450
of your choice--

00:32:50.450 --> 00:32:52.490
hey, GCP is a nice one--

00:32:52.490 --> 00:32:56.760
and make us do it for you.

00:32:56.760 --> 00:32:59.180
Now in order to go home
and try it yourself,

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:01.870
Confluent Cloud
arrives in two flavors.

00:33:01.870 --> 00:33:03.920
Confluent Cloud
Enterprise means that you

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:06.240
have to call a salesperson
and talk to them.

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:10.460
This is our unlimited-scale
enterprise level.

00:33:10.460 --> 00:33:11.400
We have SLAs.

00:33:11.400 --> 00:33:12.500
We have support.

00:33:12.500 --> 00:33:13.700
We have VPC peering.

00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:15.260
You have security options.

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:18.770
When Boerge wants to move
into Confluent Cloud,

00:33:18.770 --> 00:33:20.930
willing that maybe
he'll choose to do it,

00:33:20.930 --> 00:33:23.390
this is probably the
options that he will use.

00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:25.340
Now, if you're a team
of developers who want

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:28.610
to experiment with Kafka,
you don't need huge clusters,

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:31.250
you don't need tons of support,
you just want to try a bunch

00:33:31.250 --> 00:33:33.024
of stuff, create a
lot of clusters--

00:33:33.024 --> 00:33:34.690
pretty much what I
did here in the demo,

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:37.340
just spin up a bunch of
clusters and try stuff out--

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:42.550
Confluent Cloud Professional is
the tool for you and your team.

00:33:42.550 --> 00:33:46.880
I hope you'll all run
home, and I'll basically

00:33:46.880 --> 00:33:50.430
look at our dashboard and see
this uptick in Confluent Cloud

00:33:50.430 --> 00:33:53.758
Professional users, because
you're all trying it.

00:33:53.758 --> 00:33:54.710
That's it.

00:33:54.710 --> 00:33:58.607
We'll still be around for the
rest of the event, I guess.

00:33:58.607 --> 00:33:59.440
BOERGE SVINGEN: Yes.

00:33:59.440 --> 00:34:02.560
GWEN SHAPIRA: So if you want to
catch us with extra questions

00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:05.770
that you are shy to ask in
public, we'll be super happy.

00:34:05.770 --> 00:34:09.710
Confluent has a booth
down on [INAUDIBLE] South

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:12.820
if you want to catch
some of our cloud experts

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:14.304
who are going to be around.

00:34:14.304 --> 00:34:15.970
KIR TITIEVSKY: Thank
you all for coming.

00:34:15.970 --> 00:34:17.470
